Instead of starting a new inner loop for every row, this keeps one growing `StringBuilder`. One star is added before printing each row.

The important detail is printing after the append. That makes the first output row contain one star, the second contain two, and so forth.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern2(int n) {
        StringBuilder row = new StringBuilder();

        int current = 1;
        while (current <= n) {
            row.append('*');
            System.out.println(row);
            current++;
        }
    }
}
```

For `n = 1`, this prints just one star. For `n <= 0`, the while loop is skipped.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` due to the growing rows being printed.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current row.

A direct nested loop is enough here. The row number also tells us how many stars to print, so row 1 gets one star, row 2 gets two, and so on.

For `n = 4`, the output is:

```text
*
**
***
****
```

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern2(int n) {
        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int col = 1; col <= row; col++) {
                cout << "*";
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};
```

The loops print nothing when `n` is zero, which is also fine.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the total number of printed stars is `1 + 2 + ... + n`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

The number of stars in a row is tied to its position, so the recursive function can handle one row and then call itself for the next row. The base case stops after row `n`.

For `n = 4`, the recursive calls print rows of lengths 1, 2, 3, and 4. The empty or zero-size case returns without printing anything.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern2(int n) {
        printRows(1, n);
    }

    private void printRows(int row, int n) {
        if (row > n) {
            return;
        }

        printStars(row);
        System.out.println();
        printRows(row + 1, n);
    }

    private void printStars(int count) {
        if (count == 0) {
            return;
        }

        System.out.print("*");
        printStars(count - 1);
    }
}
```

This version is easy to match with the pattern, but recursion uses call stack space.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because every star is printed once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive calls.

## Intuition
Nested loops for incremental pattern printing



## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public void pattern2(int n) {
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
        {
            for(int j=0;j<=i;j++)
            {
                System.out.print("*");
            }

        System.out.println();
        }

    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(n^2) 

**Space Complexity:**
O(1)

